/*
 * conf/sge.config
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Execution profile for an SGE / Univa Grid Engine cluster, tuned for the
 * UCL Research Computing clusters (Myriad / Kathleen / Young).
 *
 * Derived from the nf-core institutional config for UCL Myriad by
 * Chris Wyatt (ucbtcdr@ucl.ac.uk) and Fernando Duarte (f.frutos@ucl.ac.uk):
 *   https://github.com/nf-core/configs/blob/master/conf/ucl_myriad.config
 * (nf-core/configs is MIT-licensed; this notice preserves that attribution.)
 *
 * Adapted by Nicholas Owen (n.owen@ucl.ac.uk), first for rnaseq-flow and then
 * ported here. The scheduler request format - memory per core rounded up, the
 * tmpfs request, the high-memory node rule, node-local scratch - is the same as
 * rnaseq-flow's conf/sge.config on purpose, so the two pipelines ask the cluster
 * for things in the same way. See "What differs from rnaseq-flow" below.
 *
 * Usage:
 *   nextflow run main.nf -profile conda,sge --input requests/requests_resolved.csv ...
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT GOES TO THE SCHEDULER, AND WHAT DOES NOT
 *
 * Only the derive processes are submitted. Everything else stays on the machine
 * Nextflow was launched from, for a reason each:
 *
 *   FETCH_GENOME                       label 'network': needs outbound network,
 *                                      which compute nodes often do not have.
 *   PREFLIGHT, REGISTER,               read or edit the vault's single config and
 *   REGISTER_ASSET                     use the wrapper's Python environment; pinned
 *                                      to 'local' in nextflow.config for every
 *                                      profile.
 *   DERIVE_TXOME (label 'derive')      submitted.
 *
 * So the launching machine still does the downloading and compressing, which is
 * the slow part of a run. If your site does not allow that on a login node, launch
 * the whole pipeline from somewhere that has both network access and permission to
 * work - an interactive session or a transfer node - and this split still holds.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT DIFFERS FROM rnaseq-flow's conf/sge.config
 *
 * 1. No container runtime. refdata-flow takes its tools from conda, whose
 *    environments Nextflow builds under work/conda on the launching machine. That
 *    directory is on the shared filesystem, so a compute node needs no network to
 *    use them. The singularity{} and wave{} blocks, and the beforeScript that set
 *    an Apptainer temp directory, are therefore not carried over.
 * 2. No params.max_* ceilings or resourceLimits. Those exist in rnaseq-flow
 *    because its processes scale their requests; the one process submitted here
 *    asks for a fixed 1 core, 4 GB and 2 h, far inside a standard node and the
 *    72 h single-core limit.
 * 3. No per-label tmpfs table. One value covers the one submitted process.
 *
 * Inherited unchanged from rnaseq-flow, and still needed:
 *
 *   Memory per core is rounded UP. UCL clusters take memory per slot, and
 *   Groovy's `/` on integers yields a BigDecimal, so a request that does not
 *   divide evenly would put a fraction in the qsub argument and be rejected.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

executor {
    name            = 'sge'
    queueSize       = 100        // concurrent jobs held in the scheduler
    submitRateLimit = '10/1s'    // ease pressure on the qmaster
}

process {
    executor = 'sge'

    // Parallel environment for multi-slot jobs. Nextflow emits `-pe smp <cpus>`.
    penv = 'smp'

    // Node-local scratch requested via `-l tmpfs=` below. A human transcriptome
    // and the uncompressed GTF it is cut from come to well under 2 GB together.
    ext.tmpfs = '10G'

    clusterOptions = {
        // UCL clusters request memory PER CORE; the scheduler multiplies by the
        // number of slots (-pe smp N) to get the job total. Round up, both to
        // avoid under-requesting and because a fractional value is not a valid
        // qsub argument.
        def mem_per_cpu = Math.ceil(task.memory.toMega() / task.cpus) as long
        // A standard (D) node provides 4.4 GB/core (4505 MB). A job asking for
        // more than that cannot run on one whatever its total, so ask explicitly
        // for the high-memory nodes (I/B, 41.1 GB/core) with Myriad's node-type
        // selector; without it the job would sit in the queue matching nothing.
        // Nothing submitted here crosses the threshold today. The rule is kept
        // so that the first process which does is routed rather than stranded.
        def node_class = mem_per_cpu > 4505 ? ' -ac allow=IB' : ''
        "-S /bin/bash -l mem=${mem_per_cpu}M -l tmpfs=${task.ext.tmpfs ?: '10G'}${node_class}"
    }

    // Run each submitted task on the node's local disk (backed by the tmpfs
    // requested above) instead of the shared filesystem; Nextflow stages inputs
    // in and copies outputs back automatically. Scoped to the submitted label:
    // the tasks that stay on the launching machine have no $TMPDIR from the
    // scheduler and gain nothing from it.
    withLabel: 'derive' {
        scratch = '$TMPDIR'
    }

    // Downloads stay where Nextflow was launched: see the header.
    withLabel: 'network' {
        executor = 'local'
    }
}
